<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kachari kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1250 CE – 1832 CE (approx.) · capital Khaspur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom of the Dimasa people of Assam, which moved from Dimapur to Maibang and finally Khaspur in the Barak valley.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dimasa-people">Dimasa Kacharis</a> ruled the Dhansiri valley from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dimapur">Dimapur</a>, whose carved stone pillars survive, by the 13th century. Repeated wars with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ahom-kingdom">Ahom kingdom</a>, which sacked Dimapur in 1536, pushed the kings south to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maibang">Maibang</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dima-hasao">North Cachar</a> hills, and in the 18th century to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khaspur">Khaspur</a> in the plains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cachar">Cachar</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kings ruled a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dimasa-people">Dimasa</a> society organised in clans and gradually adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> kingship; in 1790 Krishnachandra and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/govinda-chandra-narayan">Govinda Chandra</a> were formally admitted to Hindu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriya</a> status, and the kingdom was styled Heramba. Courts and land records came to use Bengali. The kingdom alternated between tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahom-people">Ahoms</a> and independence, and fought <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-manipur">Manipur</a> and the Jaintia kingdom.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Burmese invasions in the 1810s and 1820s ravaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cachar">Cachar</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/govinda-chandra-narayan">Govinda Chandra</a>, the last king, was assassinated in 1830 without an heir, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">British East India Company</a> annexed the plains in 1832. The hill areas followed later, and the region is now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1250 CE – 1832 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:84.58%;width:11.58%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1250 CE – 1832 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Khaspur</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Krishnachandra, Govinda Chandra</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Dimasa religion, Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dimasa, later Bengali</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dimasa Kingdom, Heramba kingdom, Kachari Kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1541&amp;focus=kachari-kingdom" aria-label="Kachari kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7032 1310 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kachari kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7352 1566L7494 1445M7352 1566L7364 1601"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7494 1445h0M7364 1601h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7352 1566h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7352 1566h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Khaspur. <a href="/atlas?year=1541&amp;focus=kachari-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

